{
  "mechanism": {
    "key": "collectors",
    "title": "Mirrored four-bar collectors",
    "subsystem": "intake",
    "construction": "Separate roller motors turn the exposed collecting surfaces. Deployment motors move multi-link arms; the linkage carries the roller across the frame boundary. The original binder reports X44 deployment at 79.37:1 and X60 roller drives.",
    "operating_sequence": "Deployment sets the mouth outside the bumper; rollers catch fuel from either side and move it inward. The piece must remain supported as it leaves the collector. A collector reaching its angle target is not proof a ball entered the conveyor.",
    "engineering_interpretation": "A four-bar can change roller height and fore-aft position together. Impact accommodation requires the whole linkage, transmission and hard stops to permit the intended motion. A flexible plate alone does not establish a reliable collapse mode. Two mouths also require a merge that does not oppose incoming streams.",
    "unknowns": "Exact impact hard-stop geometry, capture sensor, merge gap and roller surface speed remain unknown. Later team replies report original intake fractures and chain faults.",
    "image_ids": [
      "binder-75601cba410631181a-p8-1"
    ],
    "note_ids": [
      "tech-75601cba410631181a-1"
    ],
    "team_stated_rationale": "Read cited historical note for team rationale; the operating reconstruction below is editorial and does not establish additional team intent.",
    "transfer": "Transfer lateral acquisition only with the merge, support contact and deploy load path. Two-sided collection is not authorization for a partner-dependent passing architecture.",
    "validation_not_performed": "First inspect the missing source interface or CAD; only then test the specific capture/transfer failure that could change the architecture. No hardware experiment was performed in this upgrade.",
