        "reason": "Selected contacts and drives explained; no fabrication completeness claimed."
      },
      "operating_sequence": {
        "state": "partial",
        "value": "Deployment sets the mouth outside the bumper; rollers catch fuel from either side and move it inward. The piece must remain supported as it leaves the collector. A collector reaching its angle target is not proof a ball entered the conveyor.",
        "reason": "Editorial sequence identifies a physical route; sensing/recovery and hidden transitions remain unresolved."
      },
      "interfaces": {
        "state": "partial",
        "value": "Exact impact hard-stop geometry, capture sensor, merge gap and roller surface speed remain unknown. Later team replies report original intake fractures and chain faults.",
        "reason": "Physical dependency explained; not a dimensionally certified interface."
      },
      "packaging": {
        "state": "partial",
        "value": "Two collectors approach the chassis from opposite sides. They feed a constrained serpentine path and a fixed-yaw launcher rather than a deep open bulk hopper. The launcher structure occupies one end of the base, while the four-bar links occupy side deployment space. The binder base image shows four corner drive modules and a lightened pan. Electronics under the pan require turning the robot over and removing its protective belly sheet according to the team release; do not draw those electronics as accessible through the ball path. Battery removal direction is not established. A fixed launcher can aim with the chassis while the side collectors sweep laterally, but that is a control and route advantage, not unrestricted acquisition in every heading.",
        "reason": "Annotated source-bounded schematic, not scaled CAD."
      },
