{
  "mechanism": {
    "key": "slapdown",
    "title": "Impact-bearing collector and bumper stop",
    "subsystem": "intake",
    "construction": "A single-pivot arm uses thick plates, #35 deployment chain and powered lower contact. The source reports 2-inch and 1.25-inch rollers and a 40:1 later deployment ratio.",
    "operating_sequence": "Lowering the arm opens the mouth and deploys storage. The leading roller pulls fuel toward the lower powered contact; that contact carries it beyond the bumper into the floor. The team release says the deployed collector rests on bumper fabric.",
    "engineering_interpretation": "Impact loads run through arm, pivot attachment and the deployed stop. Using the bumper as a stop makes fabric thickness and bumper installation part of the collector geometry. A stronger chain cannot correct a mouth held too high by the bumper.",
    "unknowns": "Roller-floor gap, actual fabric compression and impact load capacity are not measured here.",
